## 1. Goals

By the end of this program I want to be able to:

| Area | Outcome |
|---|---|
| Fundamentals | Understand how computers, operating systems, networks, and servers work |
| Backend | Build production-quality backend applications |
| Data | Work confidently with relational and non-relational databases |
| Architecture | Design and evolve software architectures; explain trade-offs |
| DevOps | Containerize with Docker, build CI/CD pipelines, deploy to the cloud |
| Platform | Understand Kubernetes and distributed systems |
| Security | Apply fundamental application and infrastructure security |
| Operations | Monitor and troubleshoot production systems |
| AI | Use coding agents effectively without depending on them; understand generated code |
| Portfolio | Build and document a serious end-to-end project |

The aim is not to memorize technologies, but to design systems rather than simply implement tickets.

---

## 2. Learning Philosophy

### Fundamentals before tools

Tools change quickly; fundamentals change slowly.

- Networking before cloud networking abstractions
- Processes and Linux before containers
- Databases before ORMs
- HTTP before frameworks
- Architecture principles before microservices
- Debugging before asking AI to fix everything

### Learn by building

Each major topic contains:

```text
notes/  exercises/  labs/  mini-project/
```

The progression:

```text
Understand → Practice → Break things → Debug → Build → Document → Explain
```

If I cannot explain something in my own words, I do not consider it mastered.

### One evolving project over many toy projects

A single main project grows through stages, so architectural decisions and trade-offs become practical instead of theoretical (see [Main Project](#4-main-project)).

---

## 3. Roadmap

| # | Phase | Topics | Goal |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | **Foundations** | Linux, Git, Networking | Operate and debug a Linux machine; work safely in a team repo; find where and why communication fails |
| 2 | **Backend** | HTTP, REST, API design, auth, validation, error handling, config, DI, middleware, background jobs, caching, async, testing | Services that are understandable, testable, maintainable, deployable |
| 3 | **Databases** | SQL, keys, indexes, constraints, transactions, ACID, isolation, normalization, query optimization, migrations (PostgreSQL); NoSQL, Redis, replication, partitioning, consistency | Choose storage by requirements, not trends |
| 4 | **Architecture** | Modularity, coupling/cohesion, SOLID, layered/hexagonal/clean, modular monolith, microservices, event-driven, DDD basics | Structure systems that evolve. *Microservices are not automatically better.* |
| 5 | **System Design** | Scalability, availability, latency, load balancing, caching, queues, replication, CAP, idempotency, rate limiting, retries, circuit breakers | Turn requirements into a design and defend the trade-offs |
| 6 | **Docker** | Images, containers, Dockerfiles, layers, volumes, networks, Compose, multi-stage builds, container security | Package an application reproducibly |
| 7 | **CI/CD** | Pipelines, tests, artifacts, environments, deployment strategies, secrets, rollback, pipeline security, Jenkins | Understand the full path from commit to running app (building on existing Jenkins experience) |
| 8 | **Cloud** | Compute, networking, VMs, storage, managed databases, IAM, firewalls, DNS, load balancers, autoscaling, monitoring, cost (AWS / Azure) | Learn transferable concepts, not provider buttons |
| 9 | **Kubernetes** | Pods, Deployments, Services, ConfigMaps, Secrets, namespaces, ingress, probes, limits, scaling, rolling updates, storage, networking | Know why it exists and operate a basic app on it (not just YAML) |
| 10 | **Security** | AuthN/AuthZ, sessions, JWT, password hashing, secrets, TLS, input validation, SQLi, XSS, CSRF, SSRF, dependencies, least privilege, OWASP Top 10, threat modeling | Understand how applications and infrastructure fail. *Integrated throughout, not a final chapter.* |
| 11 | **Observability** | Logs, metrics, traces, health checks, alerting, dashboards, correlation IDs, incident investigation, performance analysis | Investigate production problems with evidence, not guesses |
| 12 | **AI Engineering** | LLM fundamentals, prompting, structured output, tool calling, embeddings, vector DBs, RAG, agents, evaluation, hallucinations, prompt injection, cost/latency | Understand how engineering changes when AI is part of the workflow |

---

## 4. Main Project

One evolving, production-style application (possible domain: e-commerce). It should eventually demonstrate authentication, users, business entities, CRUD, relational data, transactions, APIs, async processing, caching, background jobs, messaging, observability, CI/CD, Docker, cloud deployment, security, scalability, and an AI-powered feature.

### Evolution stages

**Stage 1 — Basic application** · *Focus: backend fundamentals, HTTP, APIs, SQL, testing*

```text
Client → Backend → Database
```

**Stage 2 — Modular monolith** · *Focus: modularity, boundaries, dependency management*

```text
              Backend
                 │
     ┌───────────┼───────────┐
   Users      Orders     Products
     └───────────┼───────────┘
                 ↓
             Database
```

**Stage 3 — Dockerized** · *Focus: containers, networking, configuration, persistence*

```text
Docker Compose:  Backend ──┬── Database
                           └── Redis
```

**Stage 4 — CI/CD** · *Focus: automation, quality gates, deployment*

```text
Git → CI → Tests → Build → Container image → Deployment
```

**Stage 5 — Cloud** · *Focus: cloud networking, infrastructure, security, reliability*

```text
Internet → Load balancer → Application → Database + Cache
```

**Stage 6 — Distributed components** *(only with a concrete reason)* · *Focus: messaging, eventual consistency, failure handling*

```text
API ──┬── Orders ──→ Message queue ──→ Notification service
      └── Products
```

**Stage 7 — AI feature** · *Focus: integration, evaluation, security, cost, reliability*

```text
User → Application → AI service (LLM + Retrieval + Business context)
```

